date: '2026-10-23'
authors:
- Ming Ying
why_read: Read this to understand the low-level optimizations that make database count queries and filtered search significantly faster. You will gain insight into query profiling, page access bottlenecks, and how architectural design influences search engine benchmarks.
hn_id: '50212683'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50212683
image: /infographics/01-hn-50212683.jpg
---

Executing COUNT queries over full-text indices in PostgreSQL is notoriously slow, frequently bottlenecked by relational MVCC overhead rather than inverted index traversal. When profiling term search workloads, visibility checks on the Postgres heap often account for more than 80 percent of total page accesses.

Because standard PostgreSQL requires reading and decoding the ctid for every matched tuple to ensure visibility, index scans waste precious I/O cycles verifying table rows. ParadeDB resolved this bottleneck in their search engine by introducing segment partitioning, separating immutable historical data from active write segments and drastically reducing visibility map lookups.

In benchmark tests against Wikipedia datasets with concurrent workloads, this storage layer optimization doubled throughput from 2645 to 5353 queries per second. In filtered and sorted complex search queries, cutting the heap decode step yielded performance improvements up to 66 times faster.

When optimizing analytics or search over relational databases, heap tuple visibility verification is usually the hidden ceiling on your query engine.

