title: Uuidv7 fails to guarantee monotonicity and accurate timestamps
source: hn
url: https://alexsci.com/blog/uuidv7-is-weird/
date: '2026-10-23'
authors:
- ibobev
why_read: Read this to understand why UUIDv7 does not strictly guarantee monotonic ordering or reliable timestamp accuracy under RFC 9562. You will learn the hidden trade-offs between clock drift, ordering guarantees, and uniqueness.
hn_id: '50211746'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50211746
image: /infographics/03-hn-50211746.jpg
---

Many engineering teams are switching to UUIDv7 as their default primary key format under the assumption that it solves two problems at once: primary key B-tree fragmentation and the need for a separate created_at column. However, relying on the embedded timestamp for domain logic is an operational trap.

RFC 9562 specifically allows implementations to alter the timestamp field. To prevent counter overflow or preserve strict monotonicity across rapid generations within the same millisecond, generators may advance the timestamp artificially into the future. Postgres itself warns that the extracted timestamp from uuid_extract_timestamp might not match generation time.

If you use the UUID timestamp to drive auditing, billing windows, or event sequencing, you risk subtle race conditions and arbitrary drift. Monotonicity across distributed nodes is also not guaranteed without cross-node synchronization mechanisms.

UUIDv7 remains an excellent choice for index locality and mitigating random page splits in clustered indexes. Keep a dedicated, database-managed timestamp column whenever business logic depends on temporal accuracy.

