title: Fixing numerical collapse and cancellation bugs in standard softmax
source: hn
url: https://jonbarron.info/softmaxfix/
date: '2026-10-23'
authors:
- Jonathan T. Barron
- Dan Gnanapragasam
- Michael Tschannen
why_read: Understand how standard implementations of softmax and cross-entropy harbor subtle numerical precision bugs that disrupt gradient stability. You will learn the mechanics behind softmax collapse and cancellation and how simple corrections stabilize training dynamics.
hn_id: '50216027'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50216027
image: /infographics/08-hn-50216027.jpg
---

Standard implementations of softmax and cross-entropy in modern machine learning frameworks contain two subtle numerical precision bugs that quietly degrade model training.

The first issue, softmax collapse, occurs when the target logit becomes significantly larger than competing logits. Under standard floating-point implementations, the gradient of the target logit rounds prematurely to zero once the margin crosses a precision cliff. In bfloat16, this cliff appears at a margin of roughly 5.5, while in float32 it occurs around 16.6. When this threshold is crossed, the computed gradients stop summing to zero, breaking an invariant that optimizers rely upon.

The second issue, softmax cancellation, corrupts knowledge distillation. When student logits draw close to teacher logits, standard routines produce non-zero gradients even when both vectors are identical, causing the student model to drift away unnecessarily.

By restructuring the log-sum-exp calculations and clamping edge cases properly, the revised formulations eliminate the slingshot phenomenon and stabilize grokking dynamics under weight decay.

Numerical stability at low precision requires getting the underlying algebra right rather than simply trusting framework defaults.

