A simple architectural change in how Linux handles workqueues delivers a 10 to 100 times speedup on high core-count systems.

Previously, flushing a kernel workqueue forced an iteration across every single possible CPU in the system to verify completion. On modern 128-core or 192-core dual-socket servers, this design resulted in severe bottlenecks. Filesystem operations like XFS CIL log forces flush workqueues on every commit, spending up to 64 microseconds simply walking per-CPU queues that were completely idle.

The fix by Tejun Heo alters the traversal semantics: a flush now visits only the queues that have actually been touched since the prior flush. On a 192-CPU node, flushing an idle per-CPU queue jumped from 40,000 operations per second to 4.2 million operations per second.

Scaling systems is rarely about adding raw concurrency. True scalability often comes down to pruning unneeded iteration over dormant state.

